  "question": {
    "text": "The maximum frequency for reflection of sky waves from a certain layer of the ionosphere is found to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><msup><mo>)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;is the maximum electron density at that layer of the ionosphere. On a certain day, it is observed that signals of frequencies higher than&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>MHz</mi></math> are not received by reflection from the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;layer of the ionosphere. Estimate the maximum electron densities of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;layers on that day.",
    "image": null
